{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '85,112p' paper.tex; sed -n '255,291p' paper.tex; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"For total coalition budget $q$, the empty and full masks consume two rows. Writing $n=q-2$, the source assigns\n\\begin{equation}\np=\\min\\{\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor,\\max(0,n-2(d-1))\\}\n\\label{eq:pairs}\n\\end{equation}\ncomplement pairs, with remaining rows sampled individually. Complementary rows are opposite directions in the constrained regression; pairing alone does not double contrast rank. This cap preserves potential design-direction headroom, not a guarantee of full rank or an attribution-error bound.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L89]]\n\nThe solver eliminates the last coefficient using efficiency. For interior mask $z_t$, define $a_{ti}=z_{ti}-z_{td}$ for $i<d$, $T=v(D)-v(\\varnothing)$, and $y_t=v(z_t)-v(\\varnothing)-z_{td}T$. It solves\n\\begin{equation}\n\\widehat\\phi_{1:d-1}=\\arg\\min_u\\|Au-y\\|_2^2,\n\\qquad \\widehat\\phi_d=T-\\sum_{i<d}\\widehat\\phi_i.\n\\label{eq:regression}\n\\end{equation}\nThe readable implementation uses equal-weight least squares with pseudoinverse cutoff \\texttt{rcond=1e-10}. Kernel weighting enters through the sampling distribution, not additional regression weights. Enforced efficiency is therefore a construction property, not an independent accuracy test.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L141]]\n\nTwo comparators have different evidentiary roles. The supplied original sampler uses independent kernel-size/subset draws with the same constrained solver; it is a methodological baseline but has no retained paired final attribution arrays for a numerical comparison here. The measured final comparator is \\texttt{shap.KernelExplainer}, with identity link, \\texttt{l1\\_reg=0}, and interior sample request $q-2$. Its stored vectors permit a direct descriptive comparison, but it is not the original independent sampler. Differences between complete configurations do not identify the causal effect of a single sampling component.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/baseline/src/program.py#L58]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L595]]\n\nThe source snapshot is readable and relevant to the supplied configuration, but its recorded tree identity is not independently authenticated as the exact sealed executable. Accordingly, the implementation description above is source-scoped; the results below describe retained evaluated vectors. Source resemblance and matching aggregate arithmetic do not close this identity gap.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/downstream_study/program_tree_sha256]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/program_tree_sha256]]\n\n\\subsection{Data, fitted models, and repeated-explanation protocol}\nThe public datasets are UCI German Credit \\citep{GermanCredit} and California ACSIncome constructed with \\texttt{folktables} \\citep{Ding2021}. The supplied manifest identifies the latter as the annual ACS PUMS release described in the supplement. Table~\\ref{tab:scope} gives the measured evaluation scope. Identical raw and harmonized predictor records are grouped before deterministic content-hash partitioning; the final partition is separated from development data. Hash-based assignment is not guaranteed class stratification. The supplied manifest reports no content overlap between partitions. Training, validation, and explanation pools are distinct.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/manifest.json#/partition]]\n% @evidence[[derived_summary.json#/costs]]\n\\end{table}\n\nBoth background length and budget affect work, so background size cannot be treated as a free accuracy adjustment. Reference construction and fitting are separate costs and are not folded into a per-deployment-call timing. The retained record does not establish total campaign CPU consumption; an external resource allowance is not a measured runtime. The budget rationale is consequently modest: evaluate bounded coalition allowances with recorded row counts, preserve reference costs separately, and avoid claiming that the observed wall-clock and CPU counters measure the same resource.\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\paragraph{A deployment interpretation requires an external tolerance.}\nThe audit does not identify an application-specific error tolerance, a feature-ranking margin requirement, or a user-action loss. Without these, even a high mean fidelity cannot establish that a single call is an adequate basis for a consequential prediction. The most defensible conclusion is comparative: separate the target game from its approximation, assess repeated top-feature selection and vector error, and specify the resource unit. Agreement can be relevant evidence without being sufficient evidence.\n\n\\paragraph{Nonlinear summaries require care.}\nThe bounded quality transformation compresses large discrepancies, and its average cannot be inverted to recover an average normalized error. In particular, the complement of mean quality is not a measured attribution-error fraction. Pooling across backgrounds and budgets also averages different finite games and computational conditions. A deployment decision would need the distribution relevant to its own fixed game and tolerance, not only the balanced aggregate used here. Our pooled readouts are useful for describing the retained grid, while the separated fits and budget-specific comparisons preserve distinctions that the aggregate hides. They do not identify which new input will be well explained.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L785]]\n% @evidence[[derived_summary.json#/cases]]\n\n\\paragraph{Reference quality and statistical scope.}\nExact ACSIncome references remove approximation error only for the fixed empirical interventional game. They do not validate background representativeness, preserve conditional feature dependence, or certify causal meaning. German Credit's approximate anchors may share error. The explained inputs are deliberately selected, backgrounds are nested training subsets, and only two fitted seeds are represented. Few input clusters and a small bootstrap ensemble limit interval precision. No new confidence interval across fitted models, deployment distribution, or public datasets is inferred. No isolated component ablation supports causal attribution to pairing or size stratification.\n\n\\paragraph{Provenance and completion are distinct.}\nThe final evaluation is reported as completed and qualified; the absence of a development improvement is not a failed final qualification. Nevertheless, detailed retained diagnostics remain unverified, and the supplied readable implementation is not independently authenticated as the exact sealed source. Recomputing aggregate metrics from stored vectors checks internal arithmetic only. Functional controls and deterministic replay are coarse checks, not a comprehensive execution, adaptivity, or scientific-validity audit. The record does not justify a diagnosis of an unobserved validity failure, nor does it eliminate unknown failure modes.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n\n\\paragraph{Incomplete coverage is not negative evidence.}\nThere is no controlled neural-model comparison, conditional-game comparison, background-resampling study, or cross-region/year transfer test. The original independent sampler lacks a paired final comparison. Unavailable measurements are not losses and are not filled with extrapolated values. The observed dataset-dependent comparison and negative allocation result should remain bounded by these omissions. Further empirical coverage would be needed to turn this audit into an operational reliability rule.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nA single finite-budget SHAP explanation cannot be assessed by repeat agreement alone. In the retained fixed-game audit, top-three overlap is high on average, yet reference-sensitive quality varies across datasets, fitted classifiers, and budgets. Enforced efficiency supplies even less evidence of attribution fidelity because it is built into the solver. At equal coalition count, averaging four short explanations has lower mean reference quality than one longer explanation in the evaluated allocation; the data do not establish its causal mechanism or a universal recommendation against averaging.\n\nThe useful outcome is a bounded assessment rather than a safety verdict: exact finite-game anchors where feasible, explicitly approximate anchors otherwise, separate agreement and vector-error readouts, input-cluster uncertainty, and measured work counters. Retained diagnostic provenance and source-identity limitations remain unresolved despite arithmetic consistency and reported evaluation completion. The results support closer auditing of a specified explanation configuration, not blanket acceptance or rejection of single-run SHAP in high-risk deployment.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence]]\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n@article{Lundberg2017,\n title={A Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions},\n author={Lundberg, Scott and Lee, Su-In},\n year={2017},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1705.07874},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874}\n}\n\n@article{Krishna2022,\n title={The Disagreement Problem in Explainable Machine Learning: A Practitioner's Perspective},\n author={Krishna, Satyapriya and Han, Tessa and Gu, Alex and Wu, Steven and Jabbari, Shahin and Lakkaraju, Himabindu},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.01602},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.01602}\n}\n\n@article{Yuan2022,\n title={An empirical study of the effect of background data size on the stability of SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) for deep learning models},\n author={Yuan, Han and Liu, Mingxuan and Kang, Lican and Miao, Chenkui and Wu, Ying},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2204.11351},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11351}\n}\n\n@article{Musco2024,\n title={Provably Accurate Shapley Value Estimation via Leverage Score Sampling},\n author={Musco, Christopher and Witter, R. Teal},\n year={2024},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.01917},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01917}\n}\n\n@article{Chen2020,\n title={True to the Model or True to the Data?},\n author={Chen, Hugh and Janizek, Joseph D. and Lundberg, Scott and Lee, Su-In},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.16234},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16234}\n}\n\n@article{Marx2020,\n title={Predictive Multiplicity in Classification},\n author={Marx, Charles T. and Calmon, Flavio du Pin and Ustun, Berk},\n year={2019},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1909.06677},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06677}\n}\n\n@article{Ding2021,\n title={Retiring Adult: New Datasets for Fair Machine Learning},\n author={Ding, Frances and Hardt, Moritz and Miller, John and Schmidt, Ludwig},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2108.04884},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04884}\n}\n\n@article{Gorishniy2021,\n title={Revisiting Deep Learning Models for Tabular Data},\n author={Gorishniy, Yury and Rubachev, Ivan and Khrulkov, Valentin and Babenko, Artem},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.11959},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.11959}\n}\n\n@article{Maleki2013,\n title={Bounding the Estimation Error of Sampling-based Shapley Value Approximation},\n author={Maleki, Sasan and Tran-Thanh, Long and Hines, Greg and Rahwan, Talal and Rogers, Alex},\n year={2013},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1306.4265},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.4265}\n}\n\n@article{Breiman2001,\n title={Random Forests},\n author={Breiman, Leo},\n year={2001},\n journal={Machine Learning},\n doi={10.1023/A:1010933404324}\n}\n\n@article{Friedman2001,\n title={Greedy function approximation: A gradient boosting machine.},\n author={Friedman, Jerome H.},\n year={2001},\n journal={The Annals of Statistics},\n doi={10.1214/aos/1013203451}\n}\n\n@article{Harris2020,\n title={Array programming with NumPy},\n author={Harris, Charles R. and Millman, K. Jarrod and van der Walt, Stéfan J. and Gommers, Ralf and Virtanen, Pauli and Cournapeau, David and Wieser, Eric and Taylor, Julian and Berg, Sebastian and Smith, Nathaniel J. and Kern, Robert and Picus, Matti and Hoyer, Stephan and van Kerkwijk, Marten H. and Brett, Matthew and Haldane, Allan and del Río, Jaime Fernández and Wiebe, Mark and Peterson, Pearu and Gérard-Marchant, Pierre and others},\n year={2020},\n journal={Nature},\n doi={10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2}\n}\n\n@article{Virtanen2020,\n title={SciPy 1.0: fundamental algorithms for scientific computing in Python},\n author={Virtanen, Pauli and Gommers, Ralf and Oliphant, Travis E. and Haberland, Matt and Reddy, Tyler and Cournapeau, David and Burovski, Evgeni and Peterson, Pearu and Weckesser, Warren and Bright, Jonathan and van der Walt, Stéfan J. and Brett, Matthew and Wilson, Joshua and Millman, K. Jarrod and Mayorov, Nikolay and Nelson, Andrew R. J. and Jones, Eric and Kern, Robert and Larson, Eric and Carey, C J and others},\n year={2020},\n journal={Nature Methods},\n doi={10.1038/s41592-019-0686-2}\n}\n\n@article{McKinney2010,\n title={Data Structures for Statistical Computing in Python},\n author={McKinney, Wes},\n year={2010},\n journal={Proceedings of the Python in Science Conference},\n doi={10.25080/Majora-92bf1922-00a}\n}\n\n@article{Efron1979,\n title={Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife},\n author={Efron, B.},\n year={1979},\n journal={The Annals of Statistics},\n doi={10.1214/aos/1176344552}\n}\n\n@article{DiCiccio1996,\n title={Bootstrap confidence intervals},\n author={DiCiccio, Thomas J. and Efron, Bradley},\n year={1996},\n journal={Statistical Science},\n doi={10.1214/ss/1032280214}\n}\n\n@article{Diaconis1977,\n title={Spearman's Footrule as a Measure of Disarray},\n author={Diaconis, Persi and Graham, R. L.},\n year={1977},\n journal={Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B: Statistical Methodology},\n doi={10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01624.x}\n}\n\n@article{Webber2010,\n title={A similarity measure for indefinite rankings},\n author={Webber, William and Moffat, Alistair and Zobel, Justin},\n year={2010},\n journal={ACM Transactions on Information Systems},\n doi={10.1145/1852102.1852106}\n}\n\n@article{Castro2009,\n title={Polynomial calculation of the Shapley value based on sampling},\n author={Castro, Javier and Gómez, Daniel and Tejada, Juan},\n year={2009},\n journal={Computers \\&amp; Operations Research},\n doi={10.1016/j.cor.2008.04.004}\n}\n\n@article{Shapley1953,\n title={17. A Value for n-Person Games},\n author={Shapley, L. S.},\n year={1953},\n journal={Contributions to the Theory of Games (AM-28), Volume II},\n doi={10.1515/9781400881970-018}\n}\n\n@misc{GermanCredit,\n title={Statlog (German Credit Data)},\n author={Hofmann, Hans},\n year={1994},\n howpublished={UCI Machine Learning Repository},\n doi={10.24432/C5NC77}\n}\n\n@article{Nogueira2018,\n title={On the Stability of Feature Selection Algorithms},\n author={Sarah Nogueira and Konstantinos Sechidis and Gavin Brown},\n year={2018},\n journal={Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n url={https://jmlr.org/papers/v18/17-514.html}\n}\n\n@article{Pedregosa2011,\n title={Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python},\n author={Fabian Pedregosa and Gaël Varoquaux and Alexandre Gramfort and Vincent Michel and Bertrand Thirion and Olivier Grisel and Mathieu Blondel and Peter Prettenhofer and Ron Weiss and Vincent Dubourg and Jake Vanderplas and Alexandre Passos and David Cournapeau and Matthieu Brucher and Matthieu Perrot and Édouard Duchesnay},\n year={2011},\n journal={Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n url={https://jmlr.org/papers/v12/pedregosa11a.html}\n}\n\n@article{Mitchell2022,\n title={Sampling Permutations for Shapley Value Estimation},\n author={Rory Mitchell and Joshua Cooper and Eibe Frank and Geoffrey Holmes},\n year={2022},\n journal={Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n url={https://jmlr.org/papers/v23/21-0439.html}\n}\n\n@article{Covert2021,\n title={ Improving KernelSHAP: Practical Shapley Value Estimation Using Linear Regression },\n author={Ian Covert and Su-In Lee},\n year={2021},\n journal={Proceedings of Machine Learning Research},\n url={https://proceedings.mlr.press/v130/covert21a.html}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
