{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"cat global_memory_summary.md; sed -n '1,80p' references.bib; sed -n '1,55p' references_provenance.json; sed -n '1,70p' claims_grounding.json\"","aggregated_output":"# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed Budget-Matched SHAP Reliability Audits with Exact-Game Anchors. Hypothesis: fixed-game single-run reliability varies with budget and attribution separation, while repeat agreement may hide reference error. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=No experiment or evaluated new mechanism supports a distilled empirical recommendation; the proposal remains an untested hypothesis.\n  - evidence: No recent ideas or attempts and empty authoritative JSONL on read. Bootstrap manifest reports combined_score=1.0 and correct=true. Named baseline source file was unavailable, leaving the candidate interface unknown. Alternatives considered: broad estimator tournament, adaptive certificate, and background-only perturbations. Selected exact-game references and measured prediction-row audits because they directly address same-model repeatability with observable candidate-owned controls. No experiments, evaluator runs, installations, or literature searches performed.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T16:36:50.070874+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=1.0, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 1.0, \"control_pass_fraction\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"coverage\": 1.0, \"local_accuracy_quality\": 0.999999999999984, \"matched_budget_gain\": -0.03279427307354245, \"reference_fidelity\": 0.928086123122648, \"reference_resolution_quality\": 0.991298041171165, \"repeat_agreement\": 0.9629629629629629}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed Kernel-Marginal Conditioning Portfolios for SHAP: eight outcome-blind candidate masks share a kernel-stratified size schedule and are selected by feature-symmetric conditioning at unchanged query budget. gate=updated; trigger=b; reason=The inspected candidate contract establishes a new actionable interface-limitation category, while the recorded evaluated sampler supplies no evidence of improvement.\n  - evidence: Prior attempt: combined_score=1.0, correct=true, matched_budget_gain=-0.03279427307354245, reference_fidelity=0.928086123122648, repeat_agreement=0.9629629629629629, rubric_overall_pass=false. Accessible previous candidate source confirms reset/start/observe with mask-only control and a prescribed unweighted efficiency-constrained solver; the absolute incumbent source path is unavailable. Alternatives considered: more complementary pairing, exact dataset anchors, and outcome-adaptive allocation. Chosen conditioning portfolio is feasible without extra observations, but improved fidelity remains untested and no cause of prior underperformance is identified. No new experiment, evaluator, installation or literature search performed.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T16:44:57.478241+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=1.0, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 1.0, \"control_pass_fraction\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"coverage\": 1.0, \"local_accuracy_quality\": 1.0, \"matched_budget_gain\": -0.013125176380736481, \"reference_fidelity\": 0.8719472427342251, \"reference_resolution_quality\": 0.991298041171165, \"repeat_agreement\": 0.9212962962962962}\n- 2026-11-17 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed Interaction-Alias-Aware Kernel Designs for SHAP: retain the eight-design shared-size portfolio but select by exact normalized monomial attribution errors rather than inverse-spectrum conditioning. gate=updated; trigger=d; reason=Qualify the previous conditioning-portfolio recommendation with its now-recorded evaluated non-improvement, without inferring a causal failure mechanism.\n  - evidence: Cycle 2: combined_score=1.0, correct=true, control_pass_fraction=1.0, coverage=1.0, matched_budget_gain=-0.013125176380736481, reference_fidelity=0.8719472427342251, repeat_agreement=0.9212962962962962; cycle 1 also lacked aggregate improvement and had negative matched-budget gain. Read named histories, campaign JSONL, conclusions and previous candidate sources. Alternative larger conditioning portfolios or complement pairing alone lack positive support; low-discrepancy priorities add a separate sampling mechanism; outcome adaptation and callable references exceed the one-batch API. Chosen mask-only alias scoring computes exact pairwise/three-way monomial regression errors at full contrast rank, but real-game improvement and CPU overhead remain unknown. No task program, evaluator, benchmark, numerical probe, package install or literature search was run.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-17T17:00:53.949960+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=1.0, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"combined_score\": 1.0, \"control_pass_fraction\": 1.0, \"correct\": true, \"coverage\": 1.0, \"local_accuracy_quality\": 1.0, \"matched_budget_gain\": -0.010818252140058352, \"reference_fidelity\": 0.8767845417182945, \"reference_resolution_quality\": 0.991298041171165, \"repeat_agreement\": 0.9158950617283951}\n@article{Lundberg2017,\n title={A Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions},\n author={Lundberg, Scott and Lee, Su-In},\n year={2017},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1705.07874},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874}\n}\n\n@article{Krishna2022,\n title={The Disagreement Problem in Explainable Machine Learning: A Practitioner's Perspective},\n author={Krishna, Satyapriya and Han, Tessa and Gu, Alex and Wu, Steven and Jabbari, Shahin and Lakkaraju, Himabindu},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.01602},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.01602}\n}\n\n@article{Yuan2022,\n title={An empirical study of the effect of background data size on the stability of SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) for deep learning models},\n author={Yuan, Han and Liu, Mingxuan and Kang, Lican and Miao, Chenkui and Wu, Ying},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2204.11351},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11351}\n}\n\n@article{Musco2024,\n title={Provably Accurate Shapley Value Estimation via Leverage Score Sampling},\n author={Musco, Christopher and Witter, R. Teal},\n year={2024},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.01917},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01917}\n}\n\n@article{Chen2020,\n title={True to the Model or True to the Data?},\n author={Chen, Hugh and Janizek, Joseph D. and Lundberg, Scott and Lee, Su-In},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.16234},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16234}\n}\n\n@article{Marx2020,\n title={Predictive Multiplicity in Classification},\n author={Marx, Charles T. and Calmon, Flavio du Pin and Ustun, Berk},\n year={2019},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1909.06677},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06677}\n}\n\n@article{Ding2021,\n title={Retiring Adult: New Datasets for Fair Machine Learning},\n author={Ding, Frances and Hardt, Moritz and Miller, John and Schmidt, Ludwig},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2108.04884},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04884}\n}\n\n@article{Gorishniy2021,\n title={Revisiting Deep Learning Models for Tabular Data},\n author={Gorishniy, Yury and Rubachev, Ivan and Khrulkov, Valentin and Babenko, Artem},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.11959},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.11959}\n}\n\n@article{Maleki2013,\n title={Bounding the Estimation Error of Sampling-based Shapley Value Approximation},\n author={Maleki, Sasan and Tran-Thanh, Long and Hines, Greg and Rahwan, Talal and Rogers, Alex},\n year={2013},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1306.4265},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.4265}\n}\n\n@article{Breiman2001,\n title={Random Forests},\n author={Breiman, Leo},\n year={2001},\n journal={Machine Learning},\n doi={10.1023/A:1010933404324}\n}\n\n{\n  \"generated_at\": \"2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00\",\n  \"count\": 25,\n  \"queries\": [\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 1705.07874\",\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 2202.01602\",\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 2204.11351\",\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 2410.01917\",\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 2006.16234\",\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 1909.06677\",\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 2108.04884\",\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 2106.11959\",\n    \"Exact permitted identifier 1306.4265\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1023/A:1010933404324\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1214/aos/1013203451\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1038/s41592-019-0686-2\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.25080/Majora-92bf1922-00a\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1214/aos/1176344552\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1214/ss/1032280214\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01624.x\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1145/1852102.1852106\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1016/j.cor.2008.04.004\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.1515/9781400881970-018\",\n    \"Exact DOI 10.24432/C5NC77\",\n    \"Exact known methodological title: On the Stability of Feature Selection Algorithms\",\n    \"Exact known methodological title: Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python\",\n    \"Exact known methodological title: Sampling Permutations for Shapley Value Estimation\",\n    \"Exact known methodological title: Improving KernelSHAP: Practical Shapley Value Estimation Using Linear Regression\"\n  ],\n  \"references\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.arxiv.1705.07874\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"1705.07874\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"Lundberg2017\",\n      \"title\": \"A Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions\",\n      \"year\": 2017,\n      \"venue\": \"arXiv\",\n      \"query\": \"Exact permitted identifier 1705.07874\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874\",\n      \"consulted_text\": \"Abstract: Understanding why a model makes a certain prediction can be as crucial as the prediction's accuracy in many applications. However, the highest accuracy for large modern datasets is often achieved by complex models that even experts struggle to interpret, such as ensemble or deep learning models, creating a tension between accuracy and interpretability. In response, various methods have recently been proposed to help users interpret the predictions of complex models, but it is often unclear how these methods are related and when one method is preferable over another. To address this problem, we present a unified framework for interpreting predictions, SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations). SHAP assigns each feature an importance value for a particular prediction. Its novel components include: (1) the identification of a new class of additive feature importance measures, and (2) theoretical results showing there is a unique solution in this class with a set of desirable properties. The new class unifies six existing methods, notable because several recent methods in the class lack the proposed desirable properties. Based on insights from this unification, we present new methods that show improved computational performance and/or better consistency with human intuition than previous approaches.\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"ref.arxiv.2202.01602\",\n      \"paper_id\": \"2202.01602\",\n      \"citation_key\": \"Krishna2022\",\n      \"title\": \"The Disagreement Problem in Explainable Machine Learning: A Practitioner's Perspective\",\n      \"year\": 2022,\n      \"venue\": \"arXiv\",\n      \"query\": \"Exact permitted identifier 2202.01602\",\n      \"source_url\": \"https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.01602\",\n      \"consulted_text\": \"Abstract: As various post hoc explanation methods are increasingly being leveraged to explain complex models in high-stakes settings, it becomes critical to develop a deeper understanding of whether and when the explanations output by these methods disagree with each other, and how such disagreements are resolved in practice. However, there is little to no research that provides answers to these critical questions. In this work, we formalize and study the disagreement problem in explainable machine learning. More specifically, we define the notion of disagreement between explanations, analyze how often such disagreements occur in practice, and how practitioners resolve these disagreements. We first conduct interviews with data scientists to understand what constitutes disagreement between explanations generated by different methods for the same model prediction, and introduce a novel quantitative framework to formalize this understanding. We then leverage this framework to carry out a rigorous empirical analysis with four real-world datasets, six state-of-the-art post hoc explanation methods, and six different predictive models, to measure the extent of disagreement between the explanations generated by various popular explanation methods. In addition, we carry out an online user study with data scientists to understand how they resolve the aforementioned disagreements. Our results indicate that (1) state-of-the-art explanation methods often disagree in terms of the explanations they output, and (2) machine learning practitioners often employ ad hoc heuristics when resolving such disagreements. These findings suggest that practitioners may be relying on misleading explanations when making consequential decisions. They also underscore the importance of developing principled frameworks for effectively evaluating and comparing explanations output by various explanation techniques.\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00\"\n    },\n{\n  \"claims\": [\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The reported final evaluation completed, was qualified, and reports correct=true; qualification is independent of a favorable scientific result.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.qualification.status\",\n        \"final.qualification.outcome\",\n        \"final.qualification.correct\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_locations\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status\",\n        \"paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome\",\n        \"paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.correct\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"The frozen configuration was selected without a qualifying development improvement; the development record is not final reliability evidence.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"study.investigation_outcome\",\n        \"study.selection_kind\",\n        \"study.search_score_delta\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_locations\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Detailed retained final diagnostics are marked unverified; arithmetic reconstruction is not an independent execution or adaptivity audit.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_locations\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Supplied source and sealed evaluation carry different recorded tree identities; exact executed-source identity is not authenticated.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"study.selection_kind\",\n        \"final.evidence.episodes\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_locations\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/downstream_study/program_tree_sha256\",\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/program_tree_sha256\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Aggregate top-three repeat overlap is 0.9458719135802469 and reference-fidelity quality is 0.9289530659189914, rounded in the abstract and results.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.repeat_agreement\",\n        \"final.metric.reference_fidelity\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_locations\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"claim\": \"Aggregate enforced-efficiency quality is 0.9999999999999954; it is not an attribution-accuracy certificate.\",\n      \"source_ids\": [\n        \"final.metric.local_accuracy_quality\"\n      ],\n      \"citation_keys\": [],\n      \"evidence_locations\": [\n        \"paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics/local_accuracy_quality\",\n        \"source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L825\"\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '70,86p' paper.tex; sed -n '262,290p' paper.tex; sed -n '160,177p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,78p' references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '70,86p' paper.tex; sed -n '262,290p' paper.tex; sed -n '160,177p' paper.tex; sed -n '1,78p' references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"The model's fitted preprocessing is applied after masking. Thus a categorical feature is replaced as one group rather than as separately masked indicator columns. The attribution target is the Shapley vector of this game:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\phi_i=\\sum_{S\\subseteq D\\setminus\\{i\\}}\n\\frac{|S|!(d-|S|-1)!}{d!}\\{v_{x,B}(S\\cup\\{i\\})-v_{x,B}(S)\\}.\n\\label{eq:shapley}\n\\end{equation}\nThis target is an explanation of $f$ relative to $B$, not a causal effect or a conditional expectation under the population distribution. Increasing background size changes the finite game as well as computational cost. We average results across background sizes for reporting, not because those games have identical attribution targets.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L582]]\n\nWe examine three descriptive questions: whether repeated estimates retain the same influential features; how close estimates lie to reference attribution vectors; and whether averaging several short estimates improves fidelity relative to one longer estimate at equal coalition count. A plausible rationale for averaging is variance reduction across independent streams. A competing consideration is that each short regression has fewer design rows and separately pays endpoint overhead. The retained comparison resolves only their combined measured effect; it does not isolate regression conditioning, bias, or complement pairing as the cause.\n\n\\subsection{Coalition-regression configuration and comparators}\nThe readable studied configuration samples coalition sizes according to the Shapley-kernel marginal, with uniform feature subsets conditional on size. For an interior coalition of size $s$, the size mass is proportional to $[s(d-s)]^{-1}$. The source combines complementary sizes into unordered strata with weights proportional to $2/[s(d-s)]$, except for the central stratum when $d$ is even. Randomized stratification allocates size draws, and sampled complement pairs are covered without reuse within a stratum until its finite population is exhausted. Random orientation maintains feature symmetry. These are dependent rows within an explanation, not independent coalition draws.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L49]]\n\nFor total coalition budget $q$, the empty and full masks consume two rows. Writing $n=q-2$, the source assigns\n\\begin{equation}\nThe audit does not identify an application-specific error tolerance, a feature-ranking margin requirement, or a user-action loss. Without these, even a high mean fidelity cannot establish that a single call is an adequate basis for a consequential prediction. The most defensible conclusion is comparative: separate the target game from its approximation, assess repeated top-feature selection and vector error, and specify the resource unit. Agreement can be relevant evidence without being sufficient evidence.\n\n\\paragraph{Nonlinear summaries require care.}\nThe bounded quality transformation compresses large discrepancies, and its average cannot be inverted to recover an average normalized error. In particular, the complement of mean quality is not a measured attribution-error fraction. Pooling across backgrounds and budgets also averages different finite games and computational conditions. A deployment decision would need the distribution relevant to its own fixed game and tolerance, not only the balanced aggregate used here. Our pooled readouts are useful for describing the retained grid, while the separated fits and budget-specific comparisons preserve distinctions that the aggregate hides. They do not identify which new input will be well explained.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L785]]\n% @evidence[[derived_summary.json#/cases]]\n\n\\paragraph{Reference quality and statistical scope.}\nExact ACSIncome references remove approximation error only for the fixed empirical interventional game. They do not validate background representativeness, preserve conditional feature dependence, or certify causal meaning. German Credit's approximate anchors may share error. The explained inputs are deliberately selected, backgrounds are nested training subsets, and only two fitted seeds are represented. Few input clusters and a small bootstrap ensemble limit interval precision. No new confidence interval across fitted models, deployment distribution, or public datasets is inferred. No isolated component ablation supports causal attribution to pairing or size stratification.\n\n\\paragraph{Provenance and completion are distinct.}\nThe final evaluation is reported as completed and qualified; the absence of a development improvement is not a failed final qualification. Nevertheless, detailed retained diagnostics remain unverified, and the supplied readable implementation is not independently authenticated as the exact sealed source. Recomputing aggregate metrics from stored vectors checks internal arithmetic only. Functional controls and deterministic replay are coarse checks, not a comprehensive execution, adaptivity, or scientific-validity audit. The record does not justify a diagnosis of an unobserved validity failure, nor does it eliminate unknown failure modes.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/candidate_diagnostics_trust]]\n\n\\paragraph{Incomplete coverage is not negative evidence.}\nThere is no controlled neural-model comparison, conditional-game comparison, background-resampling study, or cross-region/year transfer test. The original independent sampler lacks a paired final comparison. Unavailable measurements are not losses and are not filled with extrapolated values. The observed dataset-dependent comparison and negative allocation result should remain bounded by these omissions. Further empirical coverage would be needed to turn this audit into an operational reliability rule.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nA single finite-budget SHAP explanation cannot be assessed by repeat agreement alone. In the retained fixed-game audit, top-three overlap is high on average, yet reference-sensitive quality varies across datasets, fitted classifiers, and budgets. Enforced efficiency supplies even less evidence of attribution fidelity because it is built into the solver. At equal coalition count, averaging four short explanations has lower mean reference quality than one longer explanation in the evaluated allocation; the data do not establish its causal mechanism or a universal recommendation against averaging.\n\nThe useful outcome is a bounded assessment rather than a safety verdict: exact finite-game anchors where feasible, explicitly approximate anchors otherwise, separate agreement and vector-error readouts, input-cluster uncertainty, and measured work counters. Retained diagnostic provenance and source-identity limitations remain unresolved despite arithmetic consistency and reported evaluation completion. The results support closer auditing of a specified explanation configuration, not blanket acceptance or rejection of single-run SHAP in high-risk deployment.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence]]\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\end{equation}\nLarger values indicate less normalized error; this is a transformation, not a calibrated probability or an application acceptability threshold. Small reference norms can amplify normalized error. For ACSIncome, ordinary fidelity is $\\quality(a,\\phi^{\\rm exact})$ using enumeration of every coalition. For German Credit it is $\\{\\quality(a,r_1)+\\quality(a,r_2)\\}/2$, using two independently seeded higher-budget package estimates. Reference resolution is the separate diagnostic $\\quality(r_1,r_2)$; agreement between approximate references does not exclude shared error. The efficiency quality is $(1+|\\sum_i a_i-T|/10^{-6})^{-1}$.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L785]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L825]]\n\nThe distinction between these diagnostics is structural. Adding a zero-sum perturbation to an attribution vector preserves efficiency while potentially changing every coordinate. Likewise, perturbations that preserve the leading absolute-feature set leave top-feature overlap unchanged even when attribution magnitudes differ. These observations explain why an efficiency check or repeated feature selection cannot replace Equation~\\ref{eq:quality}. They are consequences of the metric definitions, not additional measured controls. Reference fidelity still requires a defensible target: a vector can be close to an empirical-game reference without describing conditional dependence or a causal intervention in the underlying population.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L785]]\n\nThe allocation contrast is\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta=\\quality\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\sum_{j=1}^{4}a^{\\rm additional}_{64,j},r_*\\right)\n-\\quality(a_{256,1},r_*),\n\\label{eq:allocation}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $r_*$ is exact for ACSIncome and $(r_1+r_2)/2$ for German Credit. Thus German allocation and ordinary fidelity use different, explicitly defined reference reductions. The short estimates are additional independent streams, not a subset selected from the six reported repetitions. The long estimate is the first retained long call, not the best one. Both choices spend 256 coalition rows. Repeated endpoints, distinct design coverage, and multiple-call overhead differ; equal coalition count is not equal runtime.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L840]]\n\nAll reported case means give equal weight to inputs, backgrounds, and relevant budgets; balanced case means are then averaged over datasets, families, and fitted seeds. We preserve the stored descriptive 95\\% percentile intervals obtained by resampling explained-input clusters within each fitted condition, using 200 bootstrap draws. Each cluster retains its backgrounds and repeat summaries. Bootstrap methodology \\citep{Efron1979,DiCiccio1996} does not turn these few, deliberately selected inputs into a population sample. Repeat pairs are dependent, and the two fits do not justify a population-level training-uncertainty interval. Budget-specific comparisons below have no fabricated confidence bars.\n@article{Lundberg2017,\n title={A Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions},\n author={Lundberg, Scott and Lee, Su-In},\n year={2017},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1705.07874},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874}\n}\n\n@article{Krishna2022,\n title={The Disagreement Problem in Explainable Machine Learning: A Practitioner's Perspective},\n author={Krishna, Satyapriya and Han, Tessa and Gu, Alex and Wu, Steven and Jabbari, Shahin and Lakkaraju, Himabindu},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2202.01602},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.01602}\n}\n\n@article{Yuan2022,\n title={An empirical study of the effect of background data size on the stability of SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) for deep learning models},\n author={Yuan, Han and Liu, Mingxuan and Kang, Lican and Miao, Chenkui and Wu, Ying},\n year={2022},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2204.11351},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11351}\n}\n\n@article{Musco2024,\n title={Provably Accurate Shapley Value Estimation via Leverage Score Sampling},\n author={Musco, Christopher and Witter, R. Teal},\n year={2024},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2410.01917},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01917}\n}\n\n@article{Chen2020,\n title={True to the Model or True to the Data?},\n author={Chen, Hugh and Janizek, Joseph D. and Lundberg, Scott and Lee, Su-In},\n year={2020},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2006.16234},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16234}\n}\n\n@article{Marx2020,\n title={Predictive Multiplicity in Classification},\n author={Marx, Charles T. and Calmon, Flavio du Pin and Ustun, Berk},\n year={2019},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1909.06677},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06677}\n}\n\n@article{Ding2021,\n title={Retiring Adult: New Datasets for Fair Machine Learning},\n author={Ding, Frances and Hardt, Moritz and Miller, John and Schmidt, Ludwig},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2108.04884},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04884}\n}\n\n@article{Gorishniy2021,\n title={Revisiting Deep Learning Models for Tabular Data},\n author={Gorishniy, Yury and Rubachev, Ivan and Khrulkov, Valentin and Babenko, Artem},\n year={2021},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:2106.11959},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.11959}\n}\n\n@article{Maleki2013,\n title={Bounding the Estimation Error of Sampling-based Shapley Value Approximation},\n author={Maleki, Sasan and Tran-Thanh, Long and Hines, Greg and Rahwan, Talal and Rogers, Alex},\n year={2013},\n journal={arXiv preprint arXiv:1306.4265},\n url={https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.4265}\n}\n\n@article{Breiman2001,\n title={Random Forests},\n author={Breiman, Leo},\n year={2001},\n journal={Machine Learning},\n doi={10.1023/A:1010933404324}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
