{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json,re\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor name in ['references_provenance.json','claims_grounding.json','paper_strategy.json','paper_data.json']:\n    data=json.loads(Path(name).read_text())\n    print(name, 'top-level keys:', list(data) if isinstance(data,dict) else type(data).__name__)\np=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nfor key,value in p.items():\n    if isinstance(value,list):\n        print('PROVENANCE LIST',key,len(value))\n        for entry in value:\n            if isinstance(entry,dict):\n                print({k:v for k,v in entry.items() if k not in ['consulted_text','authors']})\n                if any(s in str(entry.get('citation_key',entry.get('key',''))) for s in ['Covert','Mitchell','Chen','Lundberg','Musco']):\n                    print('CONSULTED',entry.get('consulted_text',''))\n    else:\n        print('PROVENANCE META',key, str(value)[:800])\ntext=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\ncited=sorted(set(key.strip() for group in re.findall(r'\\\\\\\\cite\\\\w*\\\\{([\"'^}]+)'\"\\\\}',text) for key in group.split(',')))\nbib=re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\{([\"'^,]+),'\"',Path('references.bib').read_text())\nprint('CITED KEYS',cited)\nprint('BIB COUNT',len(bib),'MISSING',sorted(set(cited)-set(bib)),'UNCITED',sorted(set(bib)-set(cited)))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json,re\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfor name in ['references_provenance.json','claims_grounding.json','paper_strategy.json','paper_data.json']:\n    data=json.loads(Path(name).read_text())\n    print(name, 'top-level keys:', list(data) if isinstance(data,dict) else type(data).__name__)\np=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())\nfor key,value in p.items():\n    if isinstance(value,list):\n        print('PROVENANCE LIST',key,len(value))\n        for entry in value:\n            if isinstance(entry,dict):\n                print({k:v for k,v in entry.items() if k not in ['consulted_text','authors']})\n                if any(s in str(entry.get('citation_key',entry.get('key',''))) for s in ['Covert','Mitchell','Chen','Lundberg','Musco']):\n                    print('CONSULTED',entry.get('consulted_text',''))\n    else:\n        print('PROVENANCE META',key, str(value)[:800])\ntext=Path('paper.tex').read_text()\ncited=sorted(set(key.strip() for group in re.findall(r'\\\\\\\\cite\\\\w*\\\\{([\"'^}]+)'\"\\\\}',text) for key in group.split(',')))\nbib=re.findall(r'@\\\\w+\\\\{([\"'^,]+),'\"',Path('references.bib').read_text())\nprint('CITED KEYS',cited)\nprint('BIB COUNT',len(bib),'MISSING',sorted(set(cited)-set(bib)),'UNCITED',sorted(set(bib)-set(cited)))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"references_provenance.json top-level keys: ['generated_at', 'count', 'queries', 'references']\nclaims_grounding.json top-level keys: ['claims']\npaper_strategy.json top-level keys: ['status', 'selected_prompt_family', 'recommended_title', 'central_question', 'central_thesis', 'narrative_rationale', 'prior_paper_story_status', 'edit_framing_decision', 'staged_input_impact', 'method_groups', 'results_focus', 'figure_plan', 'figure_specs', 'supplementary_plan', 'forbidden_headings', 'forbidden_phrases', 'notes_for_writer', 'failure_reason']\npaper_data.json top-level keys: ['ablation_studies', 'all_programs', 'best_any', 'best_correct', 'downstream_study', 'evidence_sources', 'final_evaluation', 'generated_at', 'grounding_sources', 'idea_titles', 'improvement_trace', 'paper_profile', 'presentation_contract', 'results_dir', 'top_programs', 'totals']\nPROVENANCE META generated_at 2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00\nPROVENANCE META count 25\nPROVENANCE LIST queries 25\nPROVENANCE LIST references 25\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.1705.07874', 'paper_id': '1705.07874', 'citation_key': 'Lundberg2017', 'title': 'A Unified Approach to Interpreting Model Predictions', 'year': 2017, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 1705.07874', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.07874', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\nCONSULTED Abstract: Understanding why a model makes a certain prediction can be as crucial as the prediction's accuracy in many applications. However, the highest accuracy for large modern datasets is often achieved by complex models that even experts struggle to interpret, such as ensemble or deep learning models, creating a tension between accuracy and interpretability. In response, various methods have recently been proposed to help users interpret the predictions of complex models, but it is often unclear how these methods are related and when one method is preferable over another. To address this problem, we present a unified framework for interpreting predictions, SHAP (SHapley Additive exPlanations). SHAP assigns each feature an importance value for a particular prediction. Its novel components include: (1) the identification of a new class of additive feature importance measures, and (2) theoretical results showing there is a unique solution in this class with a set of desirable properties. The new class unifies six existing methods, notable because several recent methods in the class lack the proposed desirable properties. Based on insights from this unification, we present new methods that show improved computational performance and/or better consistency with human intuition than previous approaches.\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.2202.01602', 'paper_id': '2202.01602', 'citation_key': 'Krishna2022', 'title': \"The Disagreement Problem in Explainable Machine Learning: A Practitioner's Perspective\", 'year': 2022, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 2202.01602', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2202.01602', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.2204.11351', 'paper_id': '2204.11351', 'citation_key': 'Yuan2022', 'title': 'An empirical study of the effect of background data size on the stability of SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) for deep learning models', 'year': 2022, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 2204.11351', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.11351', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.2410.01917', 'paper_id': '2410.01917', 'citation_key': 'Musco2024', 'title': 'Provably Accurate Shapley Value Estimation via Leverage Score Sampling', 'year': 2024, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 2410.01917', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.01917', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\nCONSULTED Abstract: Originally introduced in game theory, Shapley values have emerged as a central tool in explainable machine learning, where they are used to attribute model predictions to specific input features. However, computing Shapley values exactly is expensive: for a general model with $n$ features, $O(2^n)$ model evaluations are necessary. To address this issue, approximation algorithms are widely used. One of the most popular is the Kernel SHAP algorithm, which is model agnostic and remarkably effective in practice. At the time of our original submission, no strong non-asymptotic complexity guarantees were known for Kernel SHAP. We address this issue by introducing Leverage SHAP, a lightweight modification of Kernel SHAP that provides provably accurate Shapley value estimates with just $O(n\\log n)$ model evaluations. Our approach takes advantage of a connection between Shapley value estimation and agnostic active learning by employing leverage score sampling, a powerful regression tool. Beyond theoretical guarantees, we find that Leverage SHAP achieves an approximately $50\\%$ reduction in error compared to the highly optimized implementation of Kernel SHAP in the widely used SHAP library [Lundberg & Lee, 2017].\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.2006.16234', 'paper_id': '2006.16234', 'citation_key': 'Chen2020', 'title': 'True to the Model or True to the Data?', 'year': 2020, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 2006.16234', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16234', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\nCONSULTED Abstract: A variety of recent papers discuss the application of Shapley values, a concept for explaining coalitional games, for feature attribution in machine learning. However, the correct way to connect a machine learning model to a coalitional game has been a source of controversy. The two main approaches that have been proposed differ in the way that they condition on known features, using either (1) an interventional or (2) an observational conditional expectation. While previous work has argued that one of the two approaches is preferable in general, we argue that the choice is application dependent. Furthermore, we argue that the choice comes down to whether it is desirable to be true to the model or true to the data. We use linear models to investigate this choice. After deriving an efficient method for calculating observational conditional expectation Shapley values for linear models, we investigate how correlation in simulated data impacts the convergence of observational conditional expectation Shapley values. Finally, we present two real data examples that we consider to be representative of possible use cases for feature attribution -- (1) credit risk modeling and (2) biological discovery. We show how a different choice of value function performs better in each scenario, and how possible attributions are impacted by modeling choices.\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.1909.06677', 'paper_id': '1909.06677', 'citation_key': 'Marx2020', 'title': 'Predictive Multiplicity in Classification', 'year': 2019, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 1909.06677', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.06677', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.2108.04884', 'paper_id': '2108.04884', 'citation_key': 'Ding2021', 'title': 'Retiring Adult: New Datasets for Fair Machine Learning', 'year': 2021, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 2108.04884', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.04884', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.2106.11959', 'paper_id': '2106.11959', 'citation_key': 'Gorishniy2021', 'title': 'Revisiting Deep Learning Models for Tabular Data', 'year': 2021, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 2106.11959', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.11959', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.arxiv.1306.4265', 'paper_id': '1306.4265', 'citation_key': 'Maleki2013', 'title': 'Bounding the Estimation Error of Sampling-based Shapley Value Approximation', 'year': 2013, 'venue': 'arXiv', 'query': 'Exact permitted identifier 1306.4265', 'source_url': 'https://arxiv.org/abs/1306.4265', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1023/a:1010933404324', 'paper_id': '10.1023/A:1010933404324', 'citation_key': 'Breiman2001', 'title': 'Random Forests', 'year': 2001, 'venue': 'Machine Learning', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1023/A:1010933404324', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1023/A:1010933404324', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1214/aos/1013203451', 'paper_id': '10.1214/aos/1013203451', 'citation_key': 'Friedman2001', 'title': 'Greedy function approximation: A gradient boosting machine.', 'year': 2001, 'venue': 'The Annals of Statistics', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1214/aos/1013203451', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/aos/1013203451', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2', 'paper_id': '10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2', 'citation_key': 'Harris2020', 'title': 'Array programming with NumPy', 'year': 2020, 'venue': 'Nature', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1038/s41586-020-2649-2', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1038/s41592-019-0686-2', 'paper_id': '10.1038/s41592-019-0686-2', 'citation_key': 'Virtanen2020', 'title': 'SciPy 1.0: fundamental algorithms for scientific computing in Python', 'year': 2020, 'venue': 'Nature Methods', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1038/s41592-019-0686-2', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1038/s41592-019-0686-2', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.25080/majora-92bf1922-00a', 'paper_id': '10.25080/Majora-92bf1922-00a', 'citation_key': 'McKinney2010', 'title': 'Data Structures for Statistical Computing in Python', 'year': 2010, 'venue': 'Proceedings of the Python in Science Conference', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.25080/Majora-92bf1922-00a', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.25080/Majora-92bf1922-00a', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1214/aos/1176344552', 'paper_id': '10.1214/aos/1176344552', 'citation_key': 'Efron1979', 'title': 'Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife', 'year': 1979, 'venue': 'The Annals of Statistics', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1214/aos/1176344552', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/aos/1176344552', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1214/ss/1032280214', 'paper_id': '10.1214/ss/1032280214', 'citation_key': 'DiCiccio1996', 'title': 'Bootstrap confidence intervals', 'year': 1996, 'venue': 'Statistical Science', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1214/ss/1032280214', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1214/ss/1032280214', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01624.x', 'paper_id': '10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01624.x', 'citation_key': 'Diaconis1977', 'title': \"Spearman's Footrule as a Measure of Disarray\", 'year': 1977, 'venue': 'Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B: Statistical Methodology', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01624.x', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01624.x', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1145/1852102.1852106', 'paper_id': '10.1145/1852102.1852106', 'citation_key': 'Webber2010', 'title': 'A similarity measure for indefinite rankings', 'year': 2010, 'venue': 'ACM Transactions on Information Systems', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1145/1852102.1852106', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1145/1852102.1852106', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1016/j.cor.2008.04.004', 'paper_id': '10.1016/j.cor.2008.04.004', 'citation_key': 'Castro2009', 'title': 'Polynomial calculation of the Shapley value based on sampling', 'year': 2009, 'venue': 'Computers &amp; Operations Research', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1016/j.cor.2008.04.004', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1016/j.cor.2008.04.004', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.1515/9781400881970-018', 'paper_id': '10.1515/9781400881970-018', 'citation_key': 'Shapley1953', 'title': '17. A Value for n-Person Games', 'year': 1953, 'venue': 'Contributions to the Theory of Games (AM-28), Volume II', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.1515/9781400881970-018', 'source_url': 'https://api.crossref.org/works/10.1515/9781400881970-018', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.doi.10.24432/c5nc77', 'paper_id': '10.24432/C5NC77', 'citation_key': 'GermanCredit', 'title': 'Statlog (German Credit Data)', 'year': 1994, 'venue': 'UCI Machine Learning Repository', 'query': 'Exact DOI 10.24432/C5NC77', 'source_url': 'https://api.datacite.org/dois/10.24432/C5NC77', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.direct.Nogueira2018', 'paper_id': 'Nogueira2018', 'citation_key': 'Nogueira2018', 'title': 'On the Stability of Feature Selection Algorithms', 'year': 2018, 'venue': 'Journal of Machine Learning Research', 'query': 'Exact known methodological title: On the Stability of Feature Selection Algorithms', 'source_url': 'https://jmlr.org/papers/v18/17-514.html', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.direct.Pedregosa2011', 'paper_id': 'Pedregosa2011', 'citation_key': 'Pedregosa2011', 'title': 'Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python', 'year': 2011, 'venue': 'Journal of Machine Learning Research', 'query': 'Exact known methodological title: Scikit-learn: Machine Learning in Python', 'source_url': 'https://jmlr.org/papers/v12/pedregosa11a.html', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\n{'id': 'ref.direct.Mitchell2022', 'paper_id': 'Mitchell2022', 'citation_key': 'Mitchell2022', 'title': 'Sampling Permutations for Shapley Value Estimation', 'year': 2022, 'venue': 'Journal of Machine Learning Research', 'query': 'Exact known methodological title: Sampling Permutations for Shapley Value Estimation', 'source_url': 'https://jmlr.org/papers/v23/21-0439.html', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\nCONSULTED Game-theoretic attribution techniques based on Shapley values are used to interpret black-box machine learning models, but their exact calculation is generally NP-hard, requiring approximation methods for non-trivial models. As the computation of Shapley values can be expressed as a summation over a set of permutations, a common approach is to sample a subset of these permutations for approximation. Unfortunately, standard Monte Carlo sampling methods can exhibit slow convergence, and more sophisticated quasi-Monte Carlo methods have not yet been applied to the space of permutations. To address this, we investigate new approaches based on two classes of approximation methods and compare them empirically. First, we demonstrate quadrature techniques in a RKHS containing functions of permutations, using the Mallows kernel in combination with kernel herding and sequential Bayesian quadrature. The RKHS perspective also leads to quasi-Monte Carlo type error bounds, with a tractable discrepancy measure defined on permutations. Second, we exploit connections between the hypersphere $\\mathbb{S}^{d-2}$ and permutations to create practical algorithms for generating permutation samples with good properties. Experiments show the above techniques provide significant improvements for Shapley value estimates over existing methods, converging to a smaller RMSE in the same number of model evaluations.\n{'id': 'ref.direct.Covert2021', 'paper_id': 'Covert2021', 'citation_key': 'Covert2021', 'title': ' Improving KernelSHAP: Practical Shapley Value Estimation Using Linear Regression ', 'year': 2021, 'venue': 'Proceedings of Machine Learning Research', 'query': 'Exact known methodological title: Improving KernelSHAP: Practical Shapley Value Estimation Using Linear Regression', 'source_url': 'https://proceedings.mlr.press/v130/covert21a.html', 'retrieved_at': '2026-11-17T17:42:49.584108+00:00'}\nCONSULTED The Shapley value concept from cooperative game theory has become a popular technique for interpreting ML models, but efficiently estimating these values remains challenging, particularly in the model-agnostic setting. Here, we revisit the idea of estimating Shapley values via linear regression to understand and improve upon this approach. By analyzing the original KernelSHAP alongside a newly proposed unbiased version, we develop techniques to detect its convergence and calculate uncertainty estimates. We also find that the original version incurs a negligible increase in bias in exchange for significantly lower variance, and we propose a variance reduction technique that further accelerates the convergence of both estimators. Finally, we develop a version of KernelSHAP for stochastic cooperative games that yields fast new estimators for two global explanation methods.\nCITED KEYS ['Breiman2001', 'Castro2009', 'Chen2020', 'Covert2021', 'DiCiccio1996', 'Diaconis1977', 'Ding2021', 'Efron1979', 'Friedman2001', 'GermanCredit', 'Gorishniy2021', 'Harris2020', 'Krishna2022', 'Lundberg2017', 'Maleki2013', 'Marx2020', 'McKinney2010', 'Mitchell2022', 'Musco2024', 'Nogueira2018', 'Pedregosa2011', 'Shapley1953', 'Virtanen2020', 'Webber2010', 'Yuan2022']\nBIB COUNT 25 MISSING [] UNCITED []\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
