{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '278,321p' paper.tex; tail -n 22 memory.md; ls global_memory/*conclusions* 2>/dev/null; rg -n 'repeat|reference|citation|Kernel|SHAP' global_memory/conclusions.md 2>/dev/null; sed -n '1,100p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '278,321p' paper.tex; tail -n 22 memory.md; ls global_memory/*conclusions* 2>/dev/null; rg -n 'repeat|reference|citation|Kernel|SHAP' global_memory/conclusions.md 2>/dev/null; sed -n '1,100p' .codex/skills/memory-log/scripts/append_memory.py\"","aggregated_output":"There is no controlled neural-model comparison, conditional-game comparison, background-resampling study, or cross-region/year transfer test. The original independent sampler lacks a paired final comparison. Unavailable measurements are not losses and are not filled with extrapolated values. The observed dataset-dependent comparison and negative allocation result should remain bounded by these omissions. Further empirical coverage would be needed to turn this audit into an operational reliability rule.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nA single finite-budget SHAP explanation cannot be assessed by repeat agreement alone. In the retained fixed-game audit, top-three overlap is high on average, yet reference-sensitive quality varies across datasets, fitted classifiers, and budgets. Enforced efficiency supplies even less evidence of attribution fidelity because it is built into the solver. At equal coalition count, averaging four short explanations has lower mean reference quality than one longer explanation in the evaluated allocation; the data do not establish its causal mechanism or a universal recommendation against averaging.\n\nThe useful outcome is a bounded assessment rather than a safety verdict: exact finite-game anchors where feasible, explicitly approximate anchors otherwise, separate agreement and vector-error readouts, input-cluster uncertainty, and measured work counters. Retained diagnostic provenance and source-identity limitations remain unresolved despite arithmetic consistency and reported evaluation completion. The results support closer auditing of a specified explanation configuration, not blanket acceptance or rejection of single-run SHAP in high-risk deployment.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence]]\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\\subsection{Data provenance and deterministic partitioning}\nThe data manifest identifies German Credit's UCI archive and California's 2024 one-year ACS PUMS person-record archive. Source URLs, archive/member digests, relationship-code adaptation, and content-grouping rules are retained in \\path{source_evidence/evaluation/manifest.json}. German Credit's raw source contains 1,000 records. The ACS source count is 203,591 before the documented processing and caps. These are source counts, not the number of explained decisions. The measured final training/validation/explanation pools are those in Table~\\ref{tab:scope}.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/manifest.json#/source_urls]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/manifest.json#/source_counts]]\n\nNormalization applies Unicode NFKC, collapsed whitespace, canonical finite decimal numbers, explicit missing markers, and normalized signed zero. Predictor hashes group duplicates before partitioning; original and harmonized predictors are checked separately. A hash prefix assigns development versus final data, and another assigns training, validation, or explanation roles. The final ACS training pool is capped at 10,000 and validation/explanation pools at 3,000. This is a deterministic, source-order-capped partition, not a representative survey subsample. The ACS \\texttt{RELSHIPP}-to-\\texttt{RELP} adapter is recorded rather than silently treating the columns as identical.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/manifest.json#/normalization]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/manifest.json#/partition]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/manifest.json#/relationship_adapter]]\n\nACS players are \\texttt{AGEP, COW, SCHL, MAR, OCCP, POBP, RELP, WKHP, SEX, RAC1P}. Numeric preprocessing covers age and weekly work hours; the remaining columns are categorical. German numeric columns are the zero-indexed positions 1, 4, 7, 10, 12, 15, and 17. The German positive target is the source class coded 2; the ACS target uses the supplied \\texttt{ACSIncome} definition. No statistical conclusion about fairness across these attributes is evaluated.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L569]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L635]]\n\n\\subsection{Seeds, uncertainty, and exact aggregation}\nFitted seeds are 7301 and 9109. The source initializes sampler randomness from the fit seed and the call's stream identifier. Higher-budget package references use distinct seeded calls within each input; the deployment package call uses a separate seed offset. Exact references enumerate every subset and sum the weighted marginal contributions in Equation~\\ref{eq:shapley}. The ACS game has 10 players, hence 1,024 subsets. Enumeration uses the same empirical background and probability-scale model as the repeated estimates.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py#L126]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L612]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L662]]\n\nFor each cell, repeat agreement averages the 15 unordered pairs among six repeats at each of the two budgets. Fidelity averages the six estimates per budget and then budgets; German fidelity also averages the two reference qualities. All cell metrics are averaged equally within a fitted dataset/model condition. Since all scientific conditions are balanced, equal-weight averaging of the 12 case means reproduces the reported final metrics. The terminal replay is not included in these reductions. Its repeated inputs do not constitute new independent evidence.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[derived_summary.json#/aggregate]]\n\nThe retained bootstrap first averages backgrounds and relevant budgets for each explained input. It resamples these input-level summaries with replacement, using generator seed 812 and 200 draws, and returns the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles. We do not reuse those pooled intervals as budget-specific bars. Deterministic reconstruction in \\path{scripts/build_figures.py} agrees with all stored cell fidelity, overlap, and allocation values to floating-point precision. This establishes consistency of the recorded reductions, not independence or external verification of the inputs.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluation/evaluate_final.py#L860]]\n% @evidence[[derived_summary.json#/maximum_reconstruction_error]]\n\n- next_action: Downstream writing should implement the thesis-first strategy, retain the measured negative allocation result and evidence limitations, safely verify intended citations and protocol details, generate readable vector-first figures, and disclose LLM assistance without inventing unseen roles.\n\n### 2026-11-17T17:26:01.622350+00:00 | step=paper | scope=paper\n- summary: Confirmed independent final qualification is completed/qualified; no search winner. Central thesis remains fixed-game repeatability versus reference fidelity and a negative equal-coalition allocation contrast.\n- evidence: paper_data.json final.qualification sources and final.evidence.episodes; copied stratified-complement source inspected. Scientific evidence bundle emitted. No experiments run. The apply_patch executable was missing; a temporary compatible patch driver was installed outside the workspace.\n- next_action: Reconstruct retained summaries, build planned plots, safely curate 25-30 references, and draft within the ICLR body budget.\n\n### 2026-11-17T17:39:42.237084+00:00 | step=draft | scope=paper\n- summary: Drafted thesis-first fixed-game audit with explicit unverified-diagnostics and sealed-source identity caveats; implemented all three planned quantitative plots plus self-guarded orchestrator overview reservation.\n- evidence: All 288 retained scientific cells reconstruct overlap, fidelity and allocation within 2.23e-16; 25 safely consulted exact-identifier references replace broad-query noise. Official ICLR 2026 template retrieved directly. No model evaluations, causal component claims or unsupported original-baseline numerical comparisons added.\n- next_action: Compile, inspect rendered body geometry and figures, validate claims and evidence trails, and repair any hard errors.\n\n### 2026-11-17T17:49:57.542389+00:00 | step=verify | scope=paper\n- summary: LaTeX/BibTeX compilation, visible supplementary validation, 66-claim validation, 25-reference checks and scientific-evidence verification pass. Final paper has nine rendered body pages.\n- evidence: scientific-evidence/verification-report.json reports 112 links, zero dangling. Canonical figure wrapper is unavailable because the host src.orchestrator package is absent; local canonical JSON Schema and declared density budgets pass. Canonical compile-input wrapper falsely rejects a system TeX config symlink across two approved system trees; sanitized LaTeX/BibTeX and rendered-SI checks succeed without altering validators or system files. Overview is intentionally reserved for orchestrator rendering, with a self-guarded placeholder and empty prompt stub.\n- next_action: Record final artifact consistency, retain qualification and unverified-provenance caveats, and hand off for orchestrator concept rendering and authoritative host checks.\n\n### 2026-11-17T17:51:04.629044+00:00 | step=final | scope=paper\n- summary: paper.tex and claims_grounding.json are consistent with grounded inputs and the thesis-first strategy. The nine-body-page PDF and substantive supplement preserve the negative allocation result, independent qualified final outcome, unverified diagnostics and source-identity limitations.\n- evidence: 66 mapped claims, 25 consulted references, all 288 retained-cell reductions reconciled; final scientific-evidence verification has zero dangling references. Local canonical figure schema, layout budgets, 48 manuscript JSON pointers, rendered supplement, bibliography and claim validation pass. Full host checks and orchestrator-owned concept rendering remain explicitly pending; no new experiment was run and paper_data.json was not modified.\n- next_action: Hand off paper.tex, paper.pdf, claims_grounding.json, figure_manifest.json and reproduction notes for synchronous orchestrator concept rendering and authoritative final compile/audit.\n\nglobal_memory/conclusions.md\nglobal_memory/conclusions_history.jsonl\nglobal_memory/conclusions_other_workers.md\n1:For the documented reset/start/observe coalition-only interface, optimize mask design while preserving the prescribed constrained regression, exact query count and seed/state contract; do not plan model training, callable-based exact-reference experiments or paper-artifact generation as candidate capabilities unless an explicit interface extension supplies them. Treat repeat agreement, correctness and good contrast conditioning as insufficient evidence of improvement: tested complementary-stratification and conditioning-portfolio variants both lacked aggregate improvement and had negative matched-budget gain, without isolating a causal design defect. A feasible, unvalidated next direction is mask-only selection by exact alias errors on analytically known low-order monomial games; retain rank priority and kernel size/subset marginals, and check actual reference-sensitive evaluation because higher-order interactions and dependent design selection can defeat this surrogate.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Append validated local/global memory entries for idea workflows.\n\nTwo modes:\n\n* default (event append) — appends one memory event to ``memory.md`` (local +\n  global) plus one JSONL record to ``memory.jsonl``. Existing behavior.\n* ``--write-conclusions`` — atomically replaces ``conclusions.md`` with a\n  validated single-paragraph distilled summary and appends a record to\n  ``conclusions_history.jsonl``. Validation rejects empty input,\n  multi-paragraph input, or input over 300 words.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport fcntl\nimport json\nimport os\nimport stat\nimport sys\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Any\n\nCONCLUSIONS_WORD_LIMIT = 300\nGATE_TRIGGER_VALUES = (\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\")\n\nLOCAL_MEMORY_HEADERS = \"\"\"# Memory Log\n\n## Context\n## Idea Discoveries\n## Program Evaluations\n## Failures / Regressions\n## Next Hypotheses\n\"\"\"\n\nGLOBAL_MEMORY_HEADER = \"# Global Memory Log\\n\\n\"\n\n\ndef utc_now() -> str:\n    \"\"\"Return the current UTC timestamp string.\"\"\"\n    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).isoformat()\n\n\ndef _load_event(*, event_json: str | None, event_file: Path | None) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Load event.\"\"\"\n    if event_json:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_json)\n    elif event_file is not None:\n        parsed = json.loads(event_file.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n    else:\n        raw = input().strip()\n        if not raw:\n            raise ValueError(\"missing event payload\")\n        parsed = json.loads(raw)\n    if not isinstance(parsed, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"event payload must be a JSON object\")\n    return parsed\n\n\ndef _ensure_required_fields(event: dict[str, Any]) -> dict[str, Any]:\n    \"\"\"Ensure required fields.\"\"\"\n    normalized = dict(event)\n    if not isinstance(normalized.get(\"timestamp\"), str) or not normalized[\"timestamp\"].strip():\n        normalized[\"timestamp\"] = utc_now()\n\n    required_string_fields = (\"step\", \"scope\", \"summary\", \"evidence\", \"next_action\")\n    for key in required_string_fields:\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if not isinstance(value, str) or not value.strip():\n            raise ValueError(f\"missing required string field: {key}\")\n        normalized[key] = value.strip()\n\n    for key in (\"idea_id\", \"attempt_id\", \"program_id\"):\n        value = normalized.get(key)\n        if value is None:\n            continue\n        if not isinstance(value, str):\n            normalized[key] = str(value)\n        normalized[key] = normalized[key].strip() or None\n\n    metrics = normalized.get(\"metrics\")\n    if metrics is not None and not isinstance(metrics, dict):\n        raise ValueError(\"metrics must be an object when present\")\n    rubrics = normalized.get(\"rubrics\")\n    if rubrics is not None and not isinstance(rubrics, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"rubrics must be an array when present\")\n    tags = normalized.get(\"tags\")\n    if tags is not None:\n        if not isinstance(tags, list):\n            raise ValueError(\"tags must be an array when present\")\n        normalized[\"tags\"] = [str(item).strip() for item in tags if str(item).strip()]\n    return normalized\n\n\ndef _markdown_entry(event: dict[str, Any]) -> str:\n    \"\"\"Handle markdown entry logic for this module.\"\"\"\n    lines = [\n        f\"### {event['timestamp']} | step={event['step']} | scope={event['scope']}\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
